# Events

vpsAdmin Events are consumer-facing notification delivery history for
completed changes, operation lifecycle transitions, security observations,
and domain notifications. They are not an audit log. An Event row is retained
only when notification routing produces delivery work, so the absence of an
Event does not prove that the underlying action did not happen.

Consumers that require an external audit stream must configure an explicit
catch-all route to a durable receiver and retain the delivered events
themselves. Event Type discovery, the Event index, default notification
routes, and database retention do not provide audit completeness.

## Event kinds

Resource mutations use one event type per logical resource and completed
action:

    vps.created
    vps.updated
    vps.deleted
    dns_record.updated

There is no generic `resource.*` prefix. The event name tells consumers which
resource contract applies. Domain facts that are more specific than CRUD keep
their semantic names, for example `vps.console_opened`,
`payment.accepted`, or `outage.announced`.

Non-empty transaction chains have a correlated lifecycle:

    operation.started
    <resource and domain completion facts>
    operation.succeeded

`operation.started`, `operation.failed`, `operation.resolved`, and
`operation.succeeded` identify the chain with `operation_id`. Completion facts
contain the same `operation_id`; `operation.succeeded.result_event_ids` lists
the correlated completion facts.

A failed or fatal chain emits `operation.failed`, but never emits the deferred
resource/domain facts that would have claimed success. If chain construction
rolls back, neither the provisional objects nor `operation.started` survive.
An allowed-empty chain is treated as a synchronous operation: committed facts
are emitted without a chain lifecycle.

`transaction_chain.state_changed` remains a lower-level administrative event.
Consumers should use `operation.*` for the logical lifecycle.

## Resource payload contract

Every resource event carries:

- `resource_schema_version`: version of the common resource payload;
- `resource_name`: stable logical resource name;
- `resource_action`: `created`, `updated`, or `deleted`;
- `resource_id`: the resource's public identifier; composite primary keys are
  encoded as an object keyed by primary-key attribute;
- actor/session identifiers when available;
- `changed_fields`: sorted logical attribute names;
- `changes`: old/new value envelopes keyed by attribute.

An envelope has one of these forms:

    {"kind":"value","value":"active"}
    {"kind":"redacted"}
    {"kind":"digest","algorithm":"sha256","digest":"...","bytes":12345}

Create events contain `new` sides and delete events contain `old` sides.
Update events contain both sides when the old value was observed reliably. A
literal JSON `null` is a real value and is distinct from an omitted side.

Credential-like attributes and model-specific sensitive content are always
redacted. This includes tokens, passwords, secrets, private/key material,
authentication challenges, opaque OS-template and notification-target
configuration, notification-template text and HTML, system configuration
values, nested delivery/history payloads, transaction inputs/outputs and
confirmation changes, and VPS user-data content. Individual values larger
than 4 KiB are represented by a SHA-256 digest. The whole resource payload is
kept within 48 KiB by digesting the largest remaining inline values as needed.

The Event Type API is the authoritative contract for a resource event. Its
`resource` object describes the logical name, action, schema version, ID type,
and every attribute's type, nullability, enum choices, value policy, and
available old/new matchers. The WebUI Event Types page renders the same
contract. Enum attributes are emitted and matched by their symbolic string
names. Serialized YAML/JSON attributes are described as `json`, irrespective
of the text column used to store them. Decimal values are lossless JSON
strings, not binary floating-point numbers. Composite identifiers are JSON
objects and list their component names and types in `id_attributes`.

Generated resource types come from declarations beside mounted model-backed
HaveAPI resources, not from the set of loaded Active Record models. A resource
opts in with `resource_events`, declaring its stable product topic, audience,
and any ownership, VPS-context, logical-name, or redaction policy. Supported
default CRUD actions are inferred from the mounted action graph; explicitly
emitted non-default CRUD outcomes are listed with `additional_actions`. Only
externally meaningful mutable resources opt in. Storage placement rows,
authentication implementation rows, translations, joins, delivery internals,
accounting, telemetry, and read-only projections are not made public merely
because their models are loaded.

Account resource declarations publish roles `account, admin` and require an
explicit owner resolver. Administrative declarations publish only role
`admin`. Event Type listing is caller-specific: ordinary and support users
receive only types with the `account` role, while administrators receive the
complete public and administrative set. `category` is a stable machine topic and
`category_label` is its localized display name. The WebUI groups Event Types by
these product topics.

## Routing and querying

All event types expose common route fields such as `event_type`, `category`,
`severity`, `roles`, and `operation_id`. Resource types additionally expose
`resource_name` and `resource_action`. Exact resource types with a scalar
identifier also expose `resource_id`; composite identifiers remain available
in the event payload but are not route-matchable as one scalar value.

Selected stable scalar resource attributes can be matched as
`old_<attribute>` and `new_<attribute>`. Redacted and digest-only values are
not matchable. The exact matcher set is published by the Event Type API; it is
not inferred by consumers.

The Event index can be filtered by exact `resource_name` and
`resource_action`, but it contains delivery history rather than every emitted
fact. Resource facts use the same routing and delivery retention contract as
other public events. Configure an explicit catch-all route to a durable
external receiver when complete off-system auditing is required.

### Notification delivery actions

Delivery methods are registered implementations of
`Notifications::DeliveryActions::Base`. Each implementation owns its stable
action name, label, target kinds, queue and routing key, configuration section,
default concurrency, target validation and identity, route and optional direct
delivery planning, snapshot preparation, transport, and response
interpretation. E-mail, webhook, Telegram, and SMS implementations live in
separate files under
`api/lib/vpsadmin/api/notifications/delivery_actions/`.

The router passes an explicit `DeliveryPlanningContext` to `plan_delivery` and
the dispatcher calls the selected action directly. Delivery actions must not
depend on being evaluated as the router, dispatcher, or target model. Adding a
delivery method therefore requires one registered implementation and its
tests, not action-name branches in those three components. Duplicate action
names, queues, routing keys, or conflicting target-kind labels fail during
registration.

The shared publisher, grouping, retry, rate-limit, and dispatcher services are
separate load units under `api/lib/vpsadmin/api/notifications/`; the main
`notifications.rb` file contains shared configuration and a small compatibility
façade.

## Emitting events

Normal HaveAPI CRUD actions are captured according to the `event_policy`
declared on their action classes or inferred for standard mounted CRUD actions.
Operation entry points and non-standard actions declare their policies beside
their implementations. Synchronous facts are written only after the mutation
succeeds, in the same database transaction when the action is atomic. Recorder
state is resolved against the final persisted rows, so a rolled-back or
reverted change does not produce a false fact.

The recorder silently ignores callbacks for undeclared resources. This keeps an
operation's internal persistence details out of the consumer contract while
preserving `operation.*` lifecycle events for the accepted transaction chain.
Direct code that attempts to define or emit a CRUD event for an undeclared
model raises, so a new public contract has to be reviewed and declared
explicitly at its resource.

Emission does not unconditionally create an Event row. Public resource,
domain, and operation events are retained only when routing produces delivery
work. Do not use unconditional persistence to turn the notification subsystem
into an internal audit database.

Transaction-chain builders must use `defer_resource_event!` or
`defer_result_event!` for successful outcomes. Deferred descriptors are stored
in the existing signed final no-op transaction input and are materialized
idempotently only after the supervisor observes `done`. Do not call
`route_event!` with a past-tense success event while constructing a chain.

Security observations and runtime facts that are true immediately may be
emitted immediately. If they occur inside a transaction-chain context, include
the operation correlation without presenting them as completed mutations.

The names `<logical_resource>.created`, `.updated`, and `.deleted` are reserved
for this resource envelope. A notification or workflow event that has a
different source or payload contract must use a distinct semantic name, such
as `outage.update_reported`, `request.submitted`, or
`user.account_created`, even when it is triggered by the same operation.

Bulk SQL writes and callback-bypassing deletes must call the explicit recorder
helpers; Active Record callbacks cannot observe them. Models declare genuine
callback-bypassing delete cascades with `event_delete_cascades`. Logical
ownership and related VPS resolution are part of the resource's
`resource_events` declaration, never guessed from arbitrary associations. An
internal transaction concern that has no public resource declares its account
path on the model with `operation_event_owner` so lifecycle ownership remains
local to the concerned type.

## Adding or changing an API resource

For every POST, PUT, PATCH, or DELETE action:

1. Decide whether the model is an externally meaningful mutable API resource.
   Do not catalog an internal row, read-only projection, accounting/telemetry
   model, translation, join, authentication implementation detail, or delivery
   implementation detail.
2. For a public resource, add `resource_events` beside its HaveAPI `model`
   declaration. Select a stable topic and `account` or `admin` audience. The
   mounted default CRUD actions are inferred; use `additional_actions` only
   for CRUD outcomes emitted outside those mounted defaults. Plugin resources
   keep the declaration in the owning plugin.
3. For an account declaration, declare and test its `owner` resolver. Add a
   `vps` resolver when the event should carry VPS context. An admin declaration
   may be system-owned.
4. Confirm that inferred default CRUD classification describes every row it
   changes. Declare `event_policy` beside a custom action when it does not.
5. Declare `event_policy` beside every custom operation, authentication flow,
   callback, or background entry point. List every model it mutates. Give
   intentionally read-only, bookkeeping, transport, or domain-event policies
   a specific kind and reason.
6. Use `defer_resource_event!` for chain-backed CRUD results. Use a typed
   semantic event only when CRUD does not express the fact.
7. Call `record_many` for `update_all`, `delete_all`, counters, or other writes
   that bypass callbacks, and declare real callback-bypassing delete cascades
   with `event_delete_cascades` on the owning model.
8. Add a stable logical name when the Active Record class name is an
   implementation detail. Internal translation/join rows should normally be
   represented by their public parent resource rather than introduced as a
   new public event family.
9. Reserve `<logical_resource>.created|updated|deleted` for the versioned
   resource envelope. Give notification and workflow facts a distinct
   semantic name when their source or payload differs.
10. Treat decimal values and composite primary keys as JSON-contract cases:
   decimals are emitted as lossless strings, while composite IDs are keyed
   objects and do not expose a scalar `resource_id` route matcher.
11. Declare model-internal sensitive attributes with `event_redact`, and public
   resource-specific attributes with the `resource_events` `redact` option,
   before recording any configuration, content, credential, delivery payload,
   or opaque provider value.
12. Add specs for successful create/update/delete, rollback or chain failure,
   old/new/null values, redaction, ownership, event correlation, and Event Type
   metadata. Assert that ordinary callers cannot see admin-only types and that
   representative internal models do not produce CRUD events.

The coverage specs enumerate mutating HaveAPI actions, operation entry points,
external mutation boundaries, and callback-bypassing cascades. A new surface
must not be merged by weakening those checks or by using a broad undocumented
fallback.

Plugins own their event declarations and policies. Core vpsAdmin must not list
plugin event types directly merely to make coverage pass.
